<h2>Step 1: Build a multi-channel sequence</h2>

<p>The most effective follow-up systems do not rely on a single channel. They combine <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email</a>, <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS</a>, and task reminders to reach people wherever they are most responsive.</p>

<p>Here is why multi-channel matters. Email is great for detailed information, links, and longer messages, but open rates typically sit between 20 and 30 percent. SMS, on the other hand, boasts open rates near 98 percent, but it is best kept short and direct. Task reminders prompt you or your team to make a personal phone call at the right moment, which adds a human touch that automation alone cannot replicate.</p>

<p>A strong sequence layers all three together. Here is what a practical multi-channel follow-up looks like:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Immediate (within 5 minutes):</strong> Automated SMS confirmation plus a welcome email with next steps</li>
  <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> A value-add email with relevant content, a case study, or answers to common questions</li>
  <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> A short, friendly SMS check-in</li>
  <li><strong>Day 7:</strong> An email with social proof, testimonials, or a helpful resource</li>
  <li><strong>Day 14:</strong> A task reminder for a personal phone call, plus a brief SMS</li>
  <li><strong>Day 30:</strong> A re-engagement email acknowledging the gap, leaving the door open with no pressure</li>
</ul>

<p>Each touchpoint serves a different purpose. The immediate response builds trust and confirms you are responsive. The day 1 email adds value. The day 3 SMS catches people who were interested but distracted. The day 7 email builds credibility. The day 14 call adds warmth. And the day 30 email keeps you in their memory for when they are ready.</p>

<p>This is not about bombarding people with messages. It is about showing up consistently with something useful at each stage. If you are new to <a href="/blog/marketing-automation-getting-started">marketing automation</a>, start with just three or four steps and build from there.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Get the timing right</h2>

<p>Timing can make or break your entire sequence. Follow up too quickly and you feel desperate. Wait too long and they have already booked with someone else.</p>

<p><strong>The first five minutes are critical.</strong> Research from InsideSales found that leads contacted within five minutes are 21 times more likely to enter the sales process than those contacted after 30 minutes. That is not a typo. Twenty-one times. An automated instant response handles this perfectly. The moment someone submits a form or sends an enquiry, your system fires off a confirmation SMS and a welcome email. No delay, no reliance on you checking your phone.</p>

<p><strong>Days 1 through 3 are your warm window.</strong> The lead still remembers you. They are still in the mindset of researching or comparing options. This is when your value-adding content and friendly check-ins have the most impact. Do not waste this window with generic "just checking in" messages. Give them something worth reading.</p>

<p><strong>Days 7 through 14 are your persistence window.</strong> By now, the leads who were going to convert quickly have already done so. The ones still in your pipeline need a different approach. Social proof, a phone call, or a different angle on the problem they are trying to solve. This is where switching channels becomes especially effective.</p>

<p><strong>Day 30 and beyond is your long game.</strong> Some people need months to make a decision. A well-crafted re-engagement message at the 30-day mark says, "No pressure. We are still here if you need us." It is low effort on your part and keeps the relationship alive for future conversion.</p>

